    <h2>Which robots it talks to</h2>
    <p>
      A <a href="https://www.pollen-robotics.com">Reachy Mini Wireless</a> on your network is what Hey Reachy is for:
      its daemon runs on the robot itself, and every feature here is behind a live session with it. A
      <strong>Lite</strong> robot — the one plugged into a computer — connects to the same app, minus the few things
      that belong to that computer rather than to the robot: its Wi-Fi, its software updates and its caches. Pollen
      has not reworked the daemon for Lite yet, so expect that half to keep moving.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And you can look around without a robot at all.</strong> The Connect screen's third choice starts a
      simulator the app carries with it — the robot's own shape, moved by the same maths, on iPhone, iPad and Mac,
      with nothing to install. It has no camera, no app store and no Wi-Fi, because those belong to a machine that is
      not there. There is still no account to create.
